    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d484">

                <pb facs="0840" n="720" sameAs="frus:frus1922v01/pg720"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/4502a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in China</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Schurman</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-06-23T16:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">June 23, 1922—4
                                p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>153. Japanese Chargé d’Affaires in a public address recently delivered at
                    Baltimore referred to the withdrawal of the Japanese troops from Hankow, and
                    stated that the Japanese Government “hopes that all the other foreign troops
                    stationed in Peking and other places according to the protocol signed after the
                    Boxer rebellion of 1900<anchor n="54" xml:id="d484-f1"/> will also be withdrawn
                    as soon as order is restored in China. The Japanese Minister at Peking has
                    already been instructed <pb facs="0841" n="721" xml:id="pg721"/> to take up this
                    matter with the representatives of the Powers there”.</p>
                <p>The Department desires information as to whether you or your interested
                    colleagues have been approached on this matter by the Japanese Minister.</p>
                <p>Repeat to Tokyo as No. 60 for information only.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d484-f1" n="54" xml:id="d484-f1-content">
                <p>For final protocol, signed Sept. 7, 1901, see <ref target="frus1901China#pg_306"
